A property $\mathcal{P}$ of Boolean functions (truth tables) is:
(i) \emph{constructive} if membership in $\mathcal{P}$ is decidable efficiently (e.g.\ in $\poly(2^n)$ time on truth tables); 
(ii) \emph{large} if a non-negligible fraction of all $n$-bit functions satisfy $\mathcal{P}$; 
(iii) \emph{useful} against a circuit class $\mathcal{C}$ if no function computed by small $\mathcal{C}$-circuits satisfies $\mathcal{P}$.
Precise parameterisations vary; see Razborov--Rudich.
